The experimental material of this study was a marine platform steel heavy plate of 100 mm thickness, and its chemical composition is shown in  Table 1 
                         . On the heavy plate production line, a continuous casting slab of 360 mm in thickness was rolled to a thickness of a 100 mm steel plate. The rough rolling temperature ranges from 1080  °C  to 1050  °C , and the finish rolling temperature ranges from 810  °C  to 850  °C . The temperatures of Ac 3  and Ac 1  for the test steel were determined to be 797 °C and 714 °C, respectively ( Fig. 1 
                         ). The preparation of layered F-M steel can be divided into two stages: The first stage was the preparation of the F-M structure. Initially, a 100 mm thick steel plate was held at 900 °C and then water-cooled to room temperature. Subsequently, it undergoes intercritical quenching treatment at 780  °C  to obtain the F-M structure. Finally, tempering treatment is performed at 650  °C  to relieve stresses. The second step is to obtain the layered microstructure by warm rolling at 600  °C . A sample measuring 100 × 100 × 200 mm was extracted from an industrially produced QLT-treated heavy steel plate. The sample was subsequently maintained at 600  °C  for 1 h and subjected to warm rolling over 9 passes, resulting in a final thickness of 15 mm, which corresponds to a total thickness reduction of 85 %. A draft of 10 mm was applied per pass. The first eight passes reduced the thickness to a value near the target, while the ninth pass was employed to accurately achieve the designated final thickness of 15 mm. Subsequently, an annealing treatment was conducted at 500  °C  to relieve internal stresses ( Fig. 1 ). The two types of samples were named WR and QLT (quenching-intercritical quenching-tempering).
